@techReport{,
   abstract = {Private and Confidential 2 Energy capacity The German economy currently consumes about 580TWh of electricity per year despite only generating around 480TWh domestically. Demand is expected to reach between 680-750TWh by 2030 due to increased electrification in hard-to-abate sectors and digitalisation. The coalition plans to quadruple solar capacity to 200GW and offshore wind to 30GW, by 2030. They also say that they aim to achieve 80% renewable energy by 2030. These figures do not add up without additional unspecified generation capacity: Meeting 2030 electricity demand with an 80% renewable energy mix would entail a renewable energy output of 544-600TWh, while the projected 200GW solar and 30GW offshore wind capacity is estimated to produce ~350TWh, leaving a gap of 190-250TWh. This gap can be filled by either 45-60GW of offshore wind or 55-75GW of onshore wind by 2030. The plan seeks to address this issue by designating 2% of German land to onshore wind development, although it is unclear what the total wind capacity of this land allocation will be and how the designation will be enforced. Solar buildout will focus on residential incentives and commercial obligations for rooftop PV installation.},
