@techReport{,
   abstract = {Executive Summary COP26 underlined its role as an arena for global deliberation on climate change. The conference served as a venue for taking stock and framing strategies, but not for a ratchet up of concerted action. Moderate success was achieved in formal negotiations regarding strengthening the workings of the Paris Agreement, breaking the current five-year assessment cycle of the Nationally Determined Contributions (NDCs) by urging member states to submit strengthened NDCs prior to COP27. Many big emitters put forward new 2030 emission targets aligned with 1.5, including the US, the EU, the UK, Japan, Canada, and Korea. China did not and middle-income countries were largely overlooked in the peer pressure for new NDCs. Principles for a ruleset for the carbon market were agreed, closing an important loophole in the Paris Agreement but only partially doing away with the risk of double counting. Ad hoc alliances formed on the margins of the negotiations dominated substantive progress, exposing both converging interests and sectorial divides between key players in different domains (methane, forestry, coal, oceans). The unexpected US-China joint declaration points to a limited but politically significant strengthening of this relationship in the climate arena. Businesses attended in unprecedented numbers, showcasing a higher level of climate engagement but at the same time leading to "Davosification" of the meeting. In contrast, activists felt their access and participation in official conversations was limited. There is a growing risk to companies not pledging, or seen to be making false claims, of being targeted by activists. There were hopes that COP26 could convene a Great Leap Forward on carbon accounting and reporting. The announcement of the creation of the International Sustainability Standards Board (ISSB) indicates progress towards achieving a consistent baseline to drive integration for investors, markets, and policymakers. Most importantly, COP26 drove dynamics towards greater scrutiny of carbon reduction action with enhanced transparency and accountability at official, voluntary, and societal levels. It remains to be seen whether pledges will be followed by the harder task of implementing long-term decarbonisation plans.},
   title = {A global arena emerges}
}
