@techReport{,
   abstract = {Private and Confidential 1 ❖ Putin's demand for rouble payments raises the ante for an early disruption of Russian gas flows to Europe with possible ramifications also for oil. ❖ While EU has been preparing for this eventuality, it would provoke an energy crisis in EU countries most dependent on Russian gas. ❖ We expect a furtherance of short-term measures, ranging from demand destruction to a massive pivot towards all alternative energy sources, and of war economy-like acceleration of renewable energy infrastructure. ❖ Chancellor Scholz and key EU decision-makers still seek to avoid disruption of energy imports. Meanwhile, pressure is mounting for EU sanctions to also target Russian oil and eventually gas imports. ❖ This pressure may weaken if the conflict abates and as inflationary pressure rises, although it will continue to be nourished by intense media coverage, especially in the case of escalation. ❖ US commitment to ramp up LNG exports to EU will mostly rely on redirected supplies, likely leading to further price competition. ❖ Member states are deliberating a common purchase of gas, LNG and hydrogen and use collective political and market weight to dampen prices ❖ Draft EU regulation directing member states to fill storage facilities to 80% by November 1 st , 2022, and 90% for following years may prove hard to execute if Russian gas flows are cut before the fall. ❖ Possible EU reform of existing power price mechanism has been pushed to May, pending Commission proposal. With Iberian Peninsula being allowed special arrangement, a general reform is now less likely. ❖ Hydrogen promoted as a long-term solution for replacing gas in heavy European industries, but build-up of green hydrogen production capacity is limited and competes with other renewable energy priorities. ❖ Increasing number of war refugees arriving in EU prone to create social tensions, but joint EU resolve to cope and help Poland likely to hold up. ❖ China continues to manoeuvre Russian relations without overstepping sanctions, while India has taken advantage of cheap Russian oil prices.},
